.TH DARQ.TERMINAL 3 2026-11-16 DarqOS "DarqOS Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
darq.terminal \- ask the Terminal to do what the operator does
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B import darq.terminal as terminal
.PP
.BI "terminal.source = " name
.B terminal.state()
.B terminal.lock()
.BI terminal.notify( text )
.B terminal.windows()
.B terminal.outputs()
.BI terminal.focus( window )
.BI terminal.set_geometry( window ", " x ", " y ", " width "=0, " height =0)
.BI terminal.close( window )
.BI terminal.open_item( item_id ", lens_for=\(dq\(dq)"
.BI terminal.create_item( type_id )
.BI terminal.import_file( path )
.BI terminal.open_url( url )
.BI terminal.open_entity( window )
.B terminal.tools()
.BI terminal.run_tool( name )
.BI terminal.run_item( item_id )
.BI terminal.watch( handler ", " loop )
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B darq.terminal
is the client of the Terminal protocol
.RI ( lib/didl/terminal.didl ),
which the Terminal serves, registered as
.BR darq.terminal ,
so that a script, or the REPL, can do what the operator does with its
panels and hotkeys.
It's only what the Terminal, or the compositor it runs, knows or owns:
search is the Index's, History the History service's, and shutting
down and rebooting are system calls.
.PP
Windows are the compositor's numbers.
.BR windows ()
returns them most recently focused first, as
.B Window
records: each one's
.IR title ,
.IR app_id ,
.I geometry
(a
.B Rect
of
.IR x ,
.IR y ,
.I width
and
.IR height ,
in the output layout's coordinates),
whether it's
.IR focused ,
and, for a lens's window, the
.I item_id
it's showing, and whether that's an
.IR entity .
.PP
.BR set_geometry ()
moves a window, and asks its client for a size: a
.I width
or
.I height
of 0 keeps that as it is, and the client may not take the size asked
for.
.PP
.BR open_item (),
.BR create_item (),
.BR import_file (),
.BR open_url ()
and
.BR open_entity ()
start a lens, as the Terminal's own child, and return once its window
is shown: the window, and, but for
.BR open_item (),
the item.
Each names
.B source
as what did it: an item's type records it in History, as what created
or imported the item.
.PP
.BR watch ()
serves a
.B Watcher
handler on an event loop, and asks the Terminal to send it what
happens: windows added, removed, changed and focused, outputs changed
and removed, locking and unlocking, and tools, and packages run,
finishing.
It's heard until the returned server's port is closed.
.SH ERRORS
Each function raises
.B TerminalError
when the Terminal says no, with its
.IR status :
.B LOCKED
while the screen's locked, for all but
.BR state (),
.BR lock (),
.BR outputs ()
and
.BR watch ();
.B NOT_FOUND
for an unknown window, tool or item, or a window that isn't a lens's;
.B FAILED
with its
.I message
saying why; or
.B RUNNING
for a tool that's running already.
It raises
.B DarqError
or
.B TimeoutError
if the Terminal can't be asked.
There's no
.BR unlock ():
that needs the password.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
import darq.terminal as terminal

terminal.source = "tidy-windows"
for window in terminal.windows():
    if window.item_id:
        terminal.set_geometry(window.window, 0, 0, 800, 600)
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR didl (5),
.BR intro (7)
